@title Orders platform architecture
@subtitle Kestrel Outfitters, Commerce Platform team
@date 2026-10-01

## Overview

The Orders platform takes web orders, reserves stock and charges cards. It handles about 3200 orders per hour at peak.

## Architecture diagram

diagram orders dir=LR title="Orders platform"

orders: cust "Customers" user
orders: stripe "Stripe" ext
orders: alb "Application Load Balancer" lb
orders: osvc "Orders Service" svc "6 tasks on ECS Fargate" focus
orders: isvc "Inventory Service" svc "4 tasks on ECS Fargate"
orders: odb "Orders DB" db "RDS PostgreSQL 16 Multi-AZ"
orders: evt "Order Events" queue "SQS standard queue"
orders: ecr "ECR Registry" store
orders: group vpc "Orders VPC (10.20.0.0/16)" alb, osvc, isvc, odb
orders: group prod "Prod account" vpc, evt
orders: group shared "Shared services account" ecr
orders: cust -> alb : HTTPS
orders: alb -> osvc : /orders
orders: alb -> isvc : /inventory
orders: osvc -> odb : SQL
orders: osvc -> evt : publish
orders: evt -> isvc : poll
orders: osvc -> stripe : charge
orders: ecr -> osvc : image pull

## Components

table = Component,Runtime,Purpose; Orders Service,ECS Fargate with 6 tasks,Accepts web orders and charges cards; Inventory Service,ECS Fargate with 4 tasks,Reserves stock; Orders DB,RDS PostgreSQL 16 on db.r6g.xlarge Multi-AZ,System of record for orders; Order Events,Standard SQS queue with 14 day retention,Carries order events to Inventory Service

## Failure modes

!! An Orders DB Multi-AZ failover takes about 60 seconds. During that window, order writes fail and the storefront shows a retry banner.
